(Ord. of 12-2-80)

800:
For purposes of this article, "gross receipts" shall mean the total amount of revenue generated from the sources itemized below during the calendar year immediately preceding the date on which the tax imposed by the article is due:
(a) Banks. Items to be included in the calculation of gross receipts, with respect to banks are as follows:
(1) Interest and fees on loans, less any interest collected on those portions of loans sold and serviced for others;
(2) Interest on balances with other depository financial institutions;
(3) Interest on federal or correspondent funds sold and securities purchased under agreements of resale;
(4) Interest on other bonds, notes and debentures, excluding interest on obligations in the state or its political subdivisions and obligations on the United States;
(5) Dividends on stocks;
(6) Income from direct lease financing;
(7) Income from fiduciary activities;
(8) Service charges, commissions and fees; and
(9) Other income.
(b) Savings and loan associations. Items to be included in the calculation of gross receipts, with respect to savings and loan associations are as follows:
(1) Interest on mortgage loans, less any interest collected on those portions of loans sold in service for others;
(2) Interest on mortgages, participations or mortgage-backed securities;
(3) Interest on real estate sold on contract;
(4) Discounts on mortgage loans purchased;
(5) Interest on other loans, excluding interest on obligations of the state or its political subdivisions and obligations of the United States;
(6) Interest on dividends on investments and deposits;
(7) Loan fees;
(8) Loan servicing fees;
(9) Other fees and charges;
(10) Gross income from real estate owned operations;
(11) Net income from office building operations;
(12) Gross income from real estate held for investment;
(13) Net income from service corporations and subsidiaries;
(14) Miscellaneous operating income;
(15) Profit on sale of real estate owned, investment securities, loans and other assets; and
(16) Miscellaneous nonoperating income.
(c) Deductions. The following items shall be deducted from gross receipts calculated pursuant to paragraph (a) or (b) of this section.
(1) An amount equal to the amount of interest paid on all liabilities for the period;
(2) An amount equal to income derived from the authorized activities of any domestic international banking facility operating pursuant to O.C.G.A. article 5(a) of chapter 1 of title 7;
(3) An amount equal to any income arising from the conduct of a banking business with persons or entities located outside of the United States, its territories or possessions;
(4) An amount equal to a depository financial institution's gross income which is taxed under the tax laws of a state other than Georgia; and
(5) To the extent that any deductions are made pursuant to subparagraphs (2), (3) and (4) of this subsection, any deductions taken under subparagraph (1) of this subsection shall be reduced by the same proportion that the deductions in subparagraphs (2) through (4) of this subsection to bear to the gross receipts of the depository financial institution as calculated before making any deductions pursuant to subparagraphs (1) through (4) above.
(Ord. (8-W-83) of 9-6-83)

804:
(a) Prohibited. The practice of massage as defined in section 13-145 and massage establishments as defined in section 13-145 are prohibited within the city.
(b) Exemptions. This article shall not apply to the following individuals while engaged in the performance of the duties of their respective professions:
(1) Physicians, surgeons, chiropractors, osteopaths or physical therapists who are duly licensed to practice their respective professions in the state;
(2) Nurses who are registered under the laws of the state;
(3) Athletic directors, coaches or trainers who are affiliated with an accredited educational institution or professional sports team and whose work is limited to athletic team members;
(4) Licensed cosmetologists or barbers who provide services customarily given in such establishments; provided that such services comply with the rules and regulations set forth by the state board governing cosmetologists and barbers;
(5) Massage therapist. For the purposes of this article, a massage therapist is defined as a person who:
a. Has reached the age of 18 years.
b. Has not been convicted or pled guilty or entered a plea of nolo contendere under any federal, state or local law of any crime involving moral turpitude, illegal gambling, any felony, public indecency, misdemeanor involving any type of sexually related crime, any crime of possession, sale or distribution of illegal drugs, distribution of materials depicting nudity or sexual activity as defined under state law, criminal solicitation to commit any of these listed offenses, or attempts to commit any of these listed offenses for a period of ten years prior to the date of business license application and has been released from parole or probation.
c. Is certified as a massage therapist by the state, or by any other state of the Union, provided that any state's requirements are consistent with subsections b. and d.; or
d. Can demonstrate graduation from a school in good standing of massage therapy, consisting of at least 550 hours of a course of study consisting of a curriculum of physical culture, massage, anatomy, hydrotherapy, hygiene and other related subjects; or
e. Is certified by the National Certification Board of Therapeutic Massage and Bodywork.
(Ord. (W-11-91) of 10-8-91; Ord. (W-4-98) of 6-1-98)

831:
All participating businesses that utilize a blocked street zone for outdoor dining shall obtain and maintain commercial general liability insurance in an amount not less than one million dollars ($1,000,000.00) per occurrence and two million dollars ($2,000,000.00) aggregate. Said policy shall:
(1) Name the City of Winder, Georgia as an additional insured;
(2) Provide coverage for bodily injury, property damage, and personal injury, including incidents that occur in the designated outdoor dining area;
(3) Be in effect during all times the outdoor dining area is in use;
(4) Be issued by an insurance company licensed to do business in Georgia and acceptable to the city;
(5) Include a provision requiring thirty (30) days' written notice to the city prior to cancellation or material change in coverage.
Proof of insurance shall be submitted to the planning and development services department prior to the placement of any furniture or equipment in the blocked street zone and shall be kept current throughout the duration of use.
(Ord. (W-26-25), § I, of 12-11-25)

